WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.440
In the simplest of terms, aiming refers to&nbsp;
being able to move your crosshair from point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.680
A to point B in the most efficient way possible.&nbsp;
In other words, it means being able to control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.560
your mouse exactly how you want it to move.
If we go a little deeper, all the motions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.640
techniques required to do this can be classified&nbsp;
into three main categories: Clicking, Tracking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:24.000
and Target Switching. If you want to be even&nbsp;
more specific, you can further divide these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.480
into subcategories that mostly relate to the way&nbsp;
targets move and behave within each main category,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.040
as well as the specific mouse motions&nbsp;
you need to perform to excel at them.&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.640
Now, even though you can theoretically divide&nbsp;
aiming into different categories—and different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.760
shooter games usually cater more to one or two&nbsp;
of these at a time, such as Clicking in games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.320
like VALORANT, Tracking in Overwatch, and Target&nbsp;
Switching in Call of Duty—at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.720
aiming is one individual motor&nbsp;
skill. These categories simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.840
act as building blocks to form your true aim.
This means that even if you think your game of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.080
choice doesn’t feature much Tracking, for example,&nbsp;
practicing that aiming technique will still help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.280
you improve your mouse control and achieve new&nbsp;
levels of consistency and confidence in your aim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:05.840
that might otherwise take much longer to develop.
Finally, an integral part of any training routine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.320
is having the ability to properly track&nbsp;
your improvement, which is what makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:13.720
this training method so special! For this,&nbsp;
we’ll use the Voltaic Benchmarks to measure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.400
your progress through all the different aiming&nbsp;
categories and subcategories. Plus, it provides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.040
the additional fun incentive of earning a&nbsp;
Voltaic Rank to show off your aiming skills!&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.640
I am excited to announce the Voltaic Daily&nbsp;
Improvement Method—a week-long training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.920
regimen that covers all the different aiming&nbsp;
techniques in the most complete way possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.440
while also helping you train to achieve&nbsp;
higher ranks in the Voltaic Benchmarks!&nbsp;

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.960
The way this method works is by dedicating&nbsp;
two days of the week to each main category.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.040
This means that on Mondays and Tuesdays,&nbsp;
you cover all the Clicking subcategories;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.240
on Wednesdays and Thursdays, you cover all&nbsp;
Tracking subcategories; and on Fridays and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:52.400
Saturdays, you train all the Target Switching&nbsp;
subcategories. Finally, on Sunday, you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.600
the option to play your weakest category once&nbsp;
again, grind a specific scenario where you feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:01.760
close to getting a new high score, or simply rest!
On each of these days, you’ll find a combination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.480
of hand-picked scenarios to train all&nbsp;
the fundamental skills of each category,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:09.160
as well as variations of the Voltaic Benchmarks&nbsp;
that will help you understand and train for each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:14.360
of them—and ultimately beat your high scores. Of&nbsp;
course, the respective benchmarks of each skill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.720
are also included in the routines themselves,&nbsp;
which means that simply by playing the routines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.080
you are actively benchmarking your&nbsp;
aim and tracking your progress.&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.880
Additionally, besides being separated&nbsp;
by aiming category and day of the week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:30.000
the routines are also divided into&nbsp;
five tiers of difficulty. This way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.800
anyone—from people who are completely new to&nbsp;
aiming on mouse and keyboard to the best aimers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:38.240
in the world—can find value in these routines.&nbsp;
To know which difficulty is best suited for you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.320
all you have to do is play the Voltaic Benchmarks&nbsp;
individually and find the appropriate difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.320
as described in the document I’ve linked below. If&nbsp;
you don’t know where to start, I highly recommend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.125
playing the Novice benchmarks. Once you have your&nbsp;
overall rank, you’ll know which routine to play!&nbsp;

00:02:51.125 --> 00:02:51.202
By the way, the document is also where&nbsp;
you’ll find all the links and share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.202 --> 00:02:52.680
codes to play the routines themselves!
This project took a long time to make,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.680
but I am very happy with the result! Special&nbsp;
thanks to 4rk for all the help! I hope this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:00.000
is useful to a lot of you, and I can't&nbsp;
wait to see you on the leaderboards!&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.049
This was Lowgravity56. Take care,&nbsp;
and happy grinding my friends!

